 "constraints": [],
 "stem": "The points are $A({=h}, {=l})$, $B({=h+a}, {=l+b})$ and $C({=h-k*b}, {=l+k*a})$. Use the distance rule to prove that triangle $ABC$ is right angled at $A$, then find its area in square units.",
 "answer": "k*(a*a+b*b)//2",
 "solution": [
  "$AB=\\sqrt{({=a})^2+({=b})^2}=\\sqrt{{=a*a+b*b}}$, so $AB^2={=a*a+b*b}$.",
  "$AC=\\sqrt{({=-k*b})^2+({=k*a})^2}=\\sqrt{{=k*k*(a*a+b*b)}}$, so $AC^2={=k*k*(a*a+b*b)}$.",
  "$BC=\\sqrt{({=-k*b-a})^2+({=k*a-b})^2}$, so $BC^2={=(k*b+a)**2+(k*a-b)**2}$.",
  "$AB^2+AC^2={=a*a+b*b}+{=k*k*(a*a+b*b)}={=(1+k*k)*(a*a+b*b)}=BC^2$.",
  "By the converse of the Pythagorean theorem, triangle $ABC$ is right angled at $A$.",
  "Area $=\\frac{1}{2}\\times AB\\times AC=\\frac{1}{2}\\times\\sqrt{{=a*a+b*b}}\\times\\sqrt{{=k*k*(a*a+b*b)}}={=k*(a*a+b*b)//2}$ square units."
 ],
 "proposed_misconceptions": [],
 "notes": "Right angle at A by construction (AC is AB rotated 90 degrees and scaled by k). Evenness required so area is whole."
}
